<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siberia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Siberian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siberia is the vast region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> extending from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Ural Mountains</a> to the Pacific. Russian expansion across it began in the late sixteenth century, driven by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, and continued into the nineteenth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-siberian-railway">Trans-Siberian Railway</a> enabled military projection and the exploitation of Siberian resources.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siberia.jpg" alt="Siberia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.Bitton · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siberia_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trade linked the khanate with Russian <b>Siberia</b>, Bukhara and China: Dzungar caravans sold horses, furs and livestock in Tobolsk and other Siberian towns and, after the peace of 1739, at Qing frontier markets, while Bukharan merchants handled much of the long-distance trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Muscovy also absorbed steppe practices in taxation, postal relays and diplomacy, took Tatar princes such as those of the Qasim Khanate into its service and set the pattern for later expansion into the Volga basin and <b>Siberia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The twin occupations had terrible human costs: Soviet deportations in June 1941 and March 1949 sent tens of thousands to <b>Siberia</b>, while under German rule about 70,000 Latvian Jews were murdered, many at Rumbula near Riga in 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…IV ('the Terrible'), who reigned from 1547 to 1584, was crowned Tsar and began the eastward expansion beyond the Ural Mountains into <b>Siberia</b>, a process accelerated by the Cossack campaigns of Yermak Timofeyevich from the early 1580s that would carry Russian sovereignty to the Pacific coast by 1639.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1928 Joseph Stalin’s five-year plans transformed the republic, which held most of the Soviet Union’s industry and resources: new industrial centres such as Magnitogorsk rose in the Urals and <b>Siberia</b>, cities grew rapidly and literacy became almost universal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Russian explorers reached the Pacific at the Sea of Okhotsk in 1639, less than sixty years after Yermak’s cossacks first crossed the Urals into <b>Siberia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
